//
// Where an overlay slot may be placed, and what must be refused.
//
// Needs no hardware: these are the geometry rules, and they are the rules the
// whole mechanism rests on. A write to the granule the core is fetching from is
// silently dropped about half the time, so a layout that violates that is not a
// slow program, it is an intermittently wrong one.
//
// REQUIRES: peano
//
// The single-slot layout, spelled out. If any of these numbers change the
// mechanism has moved and every other test's assumptions need re-checking.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry one_slot | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ONE
// ONE: device npu2
// ONE: program-memory 0x4000
// ONE: host-offset 0x20000
// ONE: write-granule 0x2000
// ONE: resident-budget 0x2000
// ONE: slot a 0x2000 0x2000
//
// Ping-pong takes one granule each, so whichever slot is being written the core
// is executing from the other -- plus a bootstrap park in granule 1, which is
// where the core waits while granule 0 is written.
//
// The granule-1 slot is listed first on purpose: phase 0 uses the first slot,
// and the core has to park in the resident for it, since the park itself has
// not been written yet when the first phase begins.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry pingpong | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PP
// PP: slot b 0x2000 0x1c00
// PP-NEXT: slot a 0x400 0x1c00
// PP-NEXT: bootstrap stub 0x3c00 0x400
//
// A slot on another tile is still a valid layout; only the blockwrite's
// column/row change.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry other_tile | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TILE
// TILE: tile (1,3)
//
// Each of these violates exactly one rule, and each must be refused. --expect-invalid
// fails the test if the layout validates, so these cannot rot into no-ops.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_unaligned --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=UNALIGNED
// UNALIGNED: refused: {{.*}}does not start on a 16-byte program-memory line
//
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_past_end --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PASTEND
// PASTEND: refused: {{.*}}runs past the end of program memory
//
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_straddles_granule --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=STRADDLE
// STRADDLE: refused: {{.*}}straddles a 0x2000-byte write granule boundary
//
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_shares_executing_granule --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SHARES
// SHARES: refused: {{.*}}shares a 0x2000-byte write granule with the code the core executes
//
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_overlap --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OVERLAP
// OVERLAP: refused: {{.*}}overlaps
//
// npu1's granule has never been characterised. Refusing is the whole point: the
// half-granule behaviour was measured on npu2, and a design that silently
// assumed it held elsewhere would build clean and race on hardware.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py geometry bad_uncharacterised_device --expect-invalid | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=UNCHAR
// UNCHAR: refused: npu1 has no characterised program-memory write granule
